WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.640
When an actor leaves a hit show, it can either
be a blessing or a curse.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.380
Sometimes, write-outs are worked into a show
very nicely, like with the revolving door

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.520
on Grey's Anatomy that only adds to the drama
factor.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.320
In other cases, though, a character is so
essential that their disappearance downright

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.730
dooms the series — especially when they're
replaced by someone who doesn't have the same

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.920
appeal as the original star.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:22.920
"DUH."

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.410
Here are a few TV shows that just couldn't
recover from the loss and replacement of a

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.449
major cast member.

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:30.449
The Office

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:35.310
Steve Carell was a well-known comic force
from The Daily Show and Anchorman before he

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.000
got his biggest break with the small screen
comedy series The Office.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.010
The show was an American adaptation of the
Ricky Gervais BBC sitcom of the same name

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.170
and became wildly popular with audiences.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.000
"I made you what you are and I get nothing
back."

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.340
Carell's film career started to take off shortly
thereafter, with starring roles in movies

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.340
like The 40 Year-Old Virgin, Little Miss Sunshine,
and Crazy Stupid Love, elevating him to household

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.360
name status.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.789
So, in 2013, when Carell's contract with The
Office was up, he opted to walk away from

00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:11.930
the series even though it was still a critical
and ratings hit for NBC.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.010
The awkward, lovelorn Michael Scott finally
found love with HR director Holly, and they

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.710
moved away to Colorado.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.609
But Dunder Mifflin still needed a boss, and
that came in the form of oily, unsettling

00:01:23.609 --> 00:01:26.939
Robert California, CEO of its parent company
Sabre.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:33.380
"Hahaha, well, I will not be blackmailed by
some ineffectual, privileged, effete, soft

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.549
p---- debutante."

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:36.469
And it was all downhill from there.

00:01:36.469 --> 00:01:41.299
The show's critical favor and viewership numbers
took a nosedive, and the series finally came

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:44.639
to a close after two Carell-free seasons.

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:46.349
The X-Files

00:01:46.349 --> 00:01:51.149
For its first six seasons, The X-Files was
a huge hit for Fox and made its on-screen

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:54.799
pair of paranormal investigators international
stars.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:59.579
But prior to the start of the series' seventh
season, David Duchovny sued the network, claiming

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:04.249
that it had sold rights to reruns of the show
at a discount to its own affiliates and cheated

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:05.989
the stars out of profits.

00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:10.599
While the lawsuit was eventually settled,
it was filed right when Duchovny was negotiating

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:15.209
working on future seasons of the show, and
neither he nor Fox were keen to continue working

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:16.209
together.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:20.989
So, a deal was reached: Duchovny's character,
Fox Mulder would mysteriously disappear at

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:25.590
the end of Season 7, and would only appear
in a handful of episodes in Season 8.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.920
But Gillian Anderson's Agent Scully still
needed someone to work with in his absence,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.200
so producers auditioned dozens of actors and
eventually settled on Robert Patrick to come

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.560
in as FBI agent John Doggett.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.329
Another actress was also hired on in case
Anderson decided to leave the show after her

00:02:41.329 --> 00:02:42.709
contract ended, too.

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:46.670
It didn't much matter, though, because without
Duchovny in his regular role, viewers tuned

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.280
out.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.700
"You saved me.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.680
As difficult and as frustrating as it's been
sometimes, your goddamn strict rationalism

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.159
and science have saved me a thousand times
over."

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:59.450
8 Simple Rules for Dating My Teenage Daughter

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.329
This ABC sitcom was forced to reformat after
the unexpected death of its star.

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:09.349
8 Simple Rules was built around TV legend
John Ritter, who starred as a cranky suburban

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:13.120
dad desperately trying to rein in his wild
teenage children.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.690
Filming had just begun on the second season
of the series when Ritter fell ill on the

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.269
set and sought medical treatment.

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:23.129
He died later that day of a heart defect that
had never been diagnosed.

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:28.040
ABC announced that the show would go on — and
it did, with some necessary changes.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.349
No longer a show about a cranky dad, it became
one about a family trying to pick up the pieces

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:35.400
after the death of its patriarch.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.350
Two new stars joined the cast to fill the
void left by Ritter: cousin C.J. and Grandpa

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:41.350
Jim.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:46.219
But without Ritter, the show only lasted another
season before being shelved for good.

00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:47.219
Blue's Clues

00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:53.609
Actor/musician Steve Burns beat out roughly
1,000 other actors in 1996 for the only human

00:03:53.609 --> 00:04:00.040
role on Blue's Clues, Nickelodeon's interactive,
animation/live action hybrid show for preschoolers.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.380
Burns played the striped-shirted host and
owner of the cartoon dog named Blue — he'd

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.939
talk directly to the young viewers at home,
urging them to help solve each episode's riddle

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:11.780
or mystery by yelling things at their screens.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:16.970
In 2002, after about 100 episodes, Burns decided
it was time to leave the series.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.660
He wanted to take on other roles as well as
perform music.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.250
Nickelodeon hired actor Donovan Patton to
be the new host of Blue's Clues, playing Steve's

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.590
younger brother Joe.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.610
So as not to traumatize or confuse the show's
very young viewers, Steve carefully explained

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:36.500
that he was leaving for college, and Joe would
be looking after Blue.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.880
Patton as Joe proved to be the gentle and
affable host Blue's Clues needed, but he just

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.569
couldn't replace someone so closely associated
with the role to an army of toddlers.

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:51.229
After around 50 episodes with Patton as Joe,
Blue's Clues was canceled.

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:52.229
Scrubs

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:56.310
This comedy about medical interns desperately
trying to figure out what they were doing

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:01.690
as they worked in a hospital was a critically-acclaimed,
Emmy-nominated hit over its first few seasons

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:02.740
on NBC.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:07.319
The network dropped the show after seven seasons,
only to see ABC pick it up.

00:05:07.319 --> 00:05:12.010
The show's writers and cast prepared a true
series finale at the end of the year, only

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:16.720
for ABC to unexpectedly renew the series again
for season 9.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.610
But star Zach Braff, the focus of the show
as young doctor John Dorian, was ready to

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.650
move on to other things.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:24.810
"Onward!"

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.960
"Eagle!"

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.460
"Oh good god."

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.720
So the show was retooled and rebooted, no
longer taking place at a teaching hospital

00:05:33.729 --> 00:05:35.509
but at a medical school.

00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:39.319
Doctor Cox and Doctor Turk were now teachers
instead of active surgeons.

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:41.870
"I'm just back to teach some classes."

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:46.620
Replacing Braff as the center of the show
and narrator was Dr. Lucy Bennett, but the

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:52.349
big changes were too much to take, and the
ninth season of Scrubs was also its last.

00:05:52.349 --> 00:05:53.470
Spin City

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.599
Michael J. Fox returned to regular series
television for the first time since the end

00:05:57.599 --> 00:05:59.690
of Family Ties with Spin City.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:04.310
On the ABC sitcom, he played Mike Flaherty,
a wheeler-dealer for a New York City deputy

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.310
mayor.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.720
Two years later, Fox announced that he had
been battling Parkinson's disease and left

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.820
the show to spend more time raising funds
for research on the disease.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:18.870
Replacing him as deputy mayor was a guy named
Charlie Crawford, portrayed by Charlie Sheen.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:23.900
With Fox out, the show became more of a romantic
comedy surrounding Sheen and Heather Locklear's

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:28.520
characters, but viewership tanked shortly
after the replacement, so ABC cut the cord

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.620
on Spin City.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:34.580
"Guess you guys aren't ready for that yet.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.400
But your kids are gonna love it."

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:38.740
Designing Women

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.660
Delta Burke had not been happy on the set
of Designing Women for years.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.900
She even claimed that a producer routinely
screamed at cast members and told them they

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.730
were expendable and criticized Burke when
she gained weight.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:51.260
"Are you serious?!"

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:56.199
CBS grew so tired of Burke publicly trashing
the show's producers and the network in the

00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:00.860
press that they fired her from the show, and
co-star Jean Smart followed her out the door

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.300
shortly thereafter.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:06.289
"Every time I look over at the sofa I kinda
miss seeing her."

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:10.259
That meant that Designing Women had lost half
of its main cast, but the show was still a

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:12.800
ratings draw, so the network brought in some
replacements.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.759
"Now I know our nerves are a little bit jangly
because of the silly ol' money situation but

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:20.539
you know what we gotta do?

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:22.599
We've got to look for the light."

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:28.400
Julia Duffy arrived as Suzanne's cousin, and
Jan Hooks came in as Carlene, Charlene's sister.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.930
Despite ratings on par with previous years,
CBS fired Duffy after her sole season on the

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:39.110
series, and replaced her with Judith Ivey
as a rich Texan widow named B.J. Poteet.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.520
It didn't do much good, because CBS moved
the show to Friday nights, where it slipped

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.720
into ratings oblivion — and then cancellation.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.000
"And that…

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.990
Is the night the lights went out in Georgia"

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:55.699
That '70s Show

00:07:55.699 --> 00:08:00.831
After seven seasons of riding on a high — ratings
wise, at least — two of That 70s Show's

00:08:00.831 --> 00:08:03.600
main stars decided to step away from the series.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.599
Topher Grace decided to leave the show in
order to more fiercely pursue his blossoming

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:10.699
film career and was written out of the eighth
season altogether.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.690
Meanwhile, Ashton Kutcher decided his time
in the bell bottoms was done too, but did

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.419
appear for a few of Season 8's early episodes.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:24.000
To replace two very big holes in its cast,
producers hired Mad TV veteran Josh Meyers.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.030
His character, Randy, joins Hyde's record
store Grooves and has a brief relationship

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:30.030
with Donna.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.250
But Randy didn't have much time to mesh with
the ensemble because the decimated cast led

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.330
to diminished popularity for the show, and
it was ultimately canned at the end of the

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:39.180
season.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:44.140
"You have the right to remain BURNED."

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:45.340
Community

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:50.420
Even though Community never had a huge audience
during its five-year run on NBC, it was still

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:53.760
a critically-acclaimed comedy with a passionate
cult following.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.280
Five seasons was ultimately NBC's limit, but
internet giant Yahoo! stepped in and renewed

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.950
Community for a sixth season, intending to
position the comedy as a premiere offering

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.950
on its streaming service Yahoo!

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:06.950
Screen.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:12.020
Original regulars Joel McHale, Gillian Jacobs,
Danny Pudi, Ken Jeong, and Alison Brie followed

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:16.390
the series to Yahoo!, but the show had already
been losing cast members and left and right,

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.030
and kept losing them as it transitioned to
streaming.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:23.220
Chevy Chase, Donald Glover, and Yvette Nicole
Brown were not part of the regular cast of

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.230
Community's sixth season.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.950
New actors were added, including Keith David
and Paget Brewster.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:33.080
But it just didn't work out, and there was
no seventh season of Community on Yahoo!

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:34.980
Screen — or anywhere.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.750
Not enough people tuned in and the show didn't
generate as much advertising revenue as Yahoo!

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:40.750
had hoped.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:44.980
The revenue was so small that it effectively
led to the complete shutdown of Yahoo!

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:45.980
Screen.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:48.790
"Show may be cancelled and moved to the internet
where it turns out 10s of millions were watching

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:50.020
the whole time may not matter.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:52.870
Fake commercial may end with disclaimer gag
which may descend into vain Chuck Lorre-esque

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:54.310
rant by narcissistic creator."

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.500
Thanks for watching!

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.030
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.209
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

